  { json: { doc_id: 'KB-01', category: 'diabetes', pageContent: 'ADA 2024 Standards of Medical Care — Glycemic Targets. For most non-pregnant adults with Type 2 Diabetes, an HbA1c goal of less than 7% is appropriate. For patients with HbA1c above 9%, consider combination therapy or insulin. When HbA1c is above 7%, intensify management by adding a second-line agent such as GLP-1 receptor agonist (preferred for cardiovascular or renal benefit) or SGLT2 inhibitor. Metformin remains first-line therapy unless contraindicated. Monitor HbA1c every 3 months until stable, then every 6 months. Fasting glucose target: 80-130 mg/dL. Post-prandial glucose target: less than 180 mg/dL.', source: 'ADA Standards of Medical Care 2024', specialty: 'endocrinology' } },
  { json: { doc_id: 'KB-02', category: 'cardiovascular', pageContent: 'ACC/AHA 2023 Cardiovascular Risk Guidelines — LDL Targets. For patients with Type 2 Diabetes aged 40-75, statin therapy is recommended. LDL cholesterol target: less than 100 mg/dL for moderate risk, less than 70 mg/dL for high risk. LDL above 130 mg/dL warrants high-intensity statin therapy (atorvastatin 40-80mg or rosuvastatin 20-40mg). If LDL remains above target on maximum tolerated statin, add ezetimibe. PCSK9 inhibitors for very high-risk patients. Lifestyle: reduce saturated fat, increase soluble fiber, aerobic exercise 150 min/week.', source: 'ACC/AHA 2023 Cardiovascular Risk Guidelines', specialty: 'cardiology' } },
  { json: { doc_id: 'KB-03', category: 'hypertension', pageContent: 'JNC 8 Hypertension Guidelines. Blood pressure target for adults with diabetes: less than 130/80 mmHg. Blood pressure 130-139/80-89 mmHg (Stage 1): lifestyle modification first — DASH diet, sodium restriction below 2.3g/day, aerobic exercise, weight loss. If target not achieved in 3 months, add ACE inhibitor or ARB as first-line for diabetic patients. ACE inhibitors (lisinopril, enalapril) provide renal protection in diabetic nephropathy. Monitor potassium and creatinine after starting ACE inhibitor. Avoid NSAIDs which raise blood pressure.', source: 'JNC 8 Hypertension Guidelines', specialty: 'cardiology' } },
  { json: { doc_id: 'KB-04', category: 'metabolic', pageContent: 'WHO Metabolic Syndrome Diagnostic Criteria. Metabolic syndrome: 3 or more of waist circumference above 102cm men or 88cm women; triglycerides above 150 mg/dL; HDL below 40 mg/dL men or 50 mg/dL women; blood pressure above 130/85 mmHg; fasting glucose above 100 mg/dL. HDL below 42 mg/dL in males indicates low protective cholesterol — increase omega-3 fatty acids, exercise, reduce refined carbohydrates. Triglycerides above 168 mg/dL require dietary intervention and possible fibrate therapy.', source: 'WHO Metabolic Syndrome Criteria', specialty: 'internal_medicine' } },
  { json: { doc_id: 'KB-05', category: 'renal', pageContent: 'NKF KDIGO CKD Guidelines for Diabetic Nephropathy. Creatinine above 1.1 mg/dL in males warrants eGFR calculation. eGFR below 60 indicates CKD Stage 3 — reduce metformin dose, increase monitoring. ACE inhibitors and ARBs are renoprotective in diabetic nephropathy — first-line for hypertension with proteinuria. SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin) provide additional renal protection and are preferred second-line agents in CKD. Monitor potassium, creatinine, blood pressure every 3-6 months. Protein intake: 0.8g/kg/day.', source: 'NKF KDIGO CKD Guidelines 2023', specialty: 'nephrology' } },
  { json: { doc_id: 'KB-06', category: 'preventive', pageContent: 'USPSTF Preventive Care Recommendations for Diabetes and Cardiovascular Risk. Annual screening for diabetic retinopathy via dilated eye exam. Annual foot exam including monofilament testing for peripheral neuropathy. Dental exam every 6 months. Influenza vaccine annually. Aspirin 75-100mg daily for diabetic patients with established cardiovascular disease. Screen for depression annually. Smoking cessation — smoking increases cardiovascular risk 2-3 fold in diabetic patients.', source: 'USPSTF Preventive Care Guidelines 2024', specialty: 'preventive_medicine' } }
